  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n - Strong winds gusting 40 to 50 mph will impact much of western \n   Montana and north-central Idaho today, creating cross winds and\n   choppy lake conditions.\n\n - There is an 85 percent chance of localized wind gusts \n   exceeding 50 mph near Anaconda and a 70 percent chance around \n   Pinesdale later this afternoon, increasing the risk of isolated\n   tree damage and power outages.\n\n - A brief cooling trend brings temporary relief on Wednesday \n   before an amplified upper ridge drives temperatures back above \n   normal, followed by a surge of monsoonal moisture bringing \n   heavy rain potential to southwest Montana by Friday night.\n\nThe main weather story today is the gusty wind potential across \nwestern Montana and north-central Idaho. A strong jet stream is \nmoving directly over west-central Montana, which will cause winds \nto crank up along the mountain peaks and ridges. Looking at the \nreal-time conditions, computer models show these intense winds \nmixing all the way down to the valley floors. We have high \nconfidence that open or wind-prone areas like Anaconda (85 percent\nchance) and Pinesdale (70 percent chance) will see at least brief\ngusts of 50 mph greater.\n\nAt the same time, we expect a few isolated showers and \nthunderstorms to pop up along the Continental Divide late this \nafternoon, particularly near Glacier National Park, before moving \nsoutheast. The primary threat will be sudden, strong wind gusts \nand perhaps some small hail. Once the cold front clears the area \ntonight, winds will shift to the north and bring in a cooler air \nmass. This will knock Wednesday's high temperatures down by 5 to \n10 degrees, giving us a short but welcome break from the recent \nheat.\n\nLooking ahead to the rest of the week, weather forecast models \nare in good agreement (over 85 percent certainty) that a strong \nhigh-pressure ridge will rapidly build back over the region on \nThursday and Friday. This will quickly push afternoon temperatures\nback into the mid-to-upper 80s for western Montana and into the \nmid-90s for the lower valleys of central Idaho. This hot and dry \nstretch will be short-lived, however. By late Friday night, a \nsurge of deep monsoonal moisture will push north out of the Great \nBasin. This will shift our primary concern from high winds to \nheavy rainfall, bringing widespread showers and thunderstorms \ncapable of torrential downpours across Lemhi County and southwest \nMontana through Saturday.",
